{"name":"Sappanwood Incense","hex":"#a24f46","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sappanwood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Wood of Biancaea sappan (syn. Caesalpinia sappan), a flowering timber tree of the legume family Fabaceae that also yields medicine and red dye."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sappanwood"},{"term":"incense","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Biotic material that releases fragrant smoke when burnt, often used in religious rites or for aesthetic reasons.","The fragrant smoke released by burning incense (sense 1)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To set on fire; to inflame; to kindle; to burn.","To anger; to infuriate."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/incense"}],"entries":[{"title":"Biancaea sappan","blocks":[{"text":"Biancaea sappan is a species of flowering tree in the legume family, Fabaceae, that is native to tropical Asia. Common names in English include sappan, sappanwood and Indian redwood. It was previously ascribed to the genus Caesalpinia. Sappanwood is related to brazilwood, and was itself called brasilwood in the Middle Ages."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Caesalpinia_sappan1.jpg/330px-Caesalpinia_sappan1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biancaea_sappan"},{"title":"Incense","blocks":[{"text":"Incense is an aromatic biotic material that releases fragrant smoke when burnt. The term is used for either the material or the aroma. Incense is used for aesthetic reasons, religious worship, aromatherapy, meditation, and ceremonial reasons. It may also be used as a simple deodorant or insect repellent."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/R%C3%B6kelse_SSB_Helga_Trefaldighet_gif.gif/330px-R%C3%B6kelse_SSB_Helga_Trefaldighet_gif.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.w